      "widgets_values": [
        "## Style onto a set of cards, structure held\n\nEach card keeps its own composition; one reference image lends the look to all of them.\n\n**Top lane \u2014 FLUX.2 [pro].** *Load Images From Directory* emits a **list**, so the [pro] node fires once per card. The card goes into `input_image` (the thing being redrawn) and the style reference into `input_image_2`. Slot order is the whole instruction here: the prompt names which is which, so say it explicitly and keep it consistent.\n\n`disable_pup` is **on**. Prompt upsampling rewrites your wording, and a style prompt you have tuned over several runs is exactly the thing you do not want rewritten.\n\nThe `filenames` output is wired to the save node's `label`, so `the-tower.png` comes back as `styled_the-tower.png` rather than `styled_0007.png`. That is what makes a run reversible \u2014 you can always tell which card a result came from.\n\n**Bottom lane \u2014 Stability Style Transfer.** The same two images with explicit dials instead of a prompt:\n\n- `style_strength` \u2014 how much of the reference's look lands.\n- `composition_fidelity` \u2014 how hard the original composition is held. This is the one to raise when a card's structure starts drifting.\n- `change_strength` \u2014 how far from the init image it may travel at all.\n\nRun both on one card first and compare. They fail differently: FLUX.2 tends to reinterpret, Stability tends to recolour.\n\n### Cost\nOne API call per card, per lane. Point `directory` at three cards before pointing it at seventy-eight."
      ],
      "title": "Read me \u2014 style transfer",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
